-- The GB gas day, a nomination, and cash-out: one rulebook that decides
-- what may happen and explains, when late evidence arrives, what changed.
--
-- A quick tour of the domain. Gas in Great Britain is traded and moved
-- by the "gas day", which runs from 05:00 to 05:00 London time. In
-- winter that is 05:00 UTC; in summer 04:00 UTC; and the days the clocks
-- change last 23 or 25 hours. A system that fixes the day at 05:00 UTC
-- misplaces an hour of every summer day.
--
-- A shipper tells the network, ahead of time, how much gas it will take
-- out at its sites: a "nomination". For daily-metered sites it is due by
-- 13:00 London time on the day before the gas day. What counts is when
-- the network's own system received it, not when anyone later decided
-- about it.
--
-- After the day, each shipper's imbalance (what it put in minus what it
-- took out) is "cashed out". The price starts from the System Average
-- Price (SAP): the average price of the day's balancing trades,
-- weighted by quantity. A day with no trades takes the mean of the
-- seven days before it, so quiet days lean on earlier ones, which may
-- themselves have leaned on earlier ones. A short shipper buys its
-- shortfall at the System Marginal Buy Price, SAP plus a small default
-- margin, or National Gas's own highest buy price that day if that is
-- higher. A long shipper sells its surplus at the Sell price, the mirror
-- image.
--
-- The rules are the Uniform Network Code (UNC): TPD Sections C and F
-- v6.27 of 14 September 2026, TPD Section D and General Terms Section C
-- v6.28 of 9 November 2026. Where the code is silent, the comment says so and names the
-- reading this example takes.
--
-- Here is how Morpholog thinks about it. It stores *claims* it has
-- agreed to admit, never "facts". An `invariant` is a rule no change may
-- break; a `require` is a gate on one act, checked when someone acts and
-- never again. The interesting part comes after the day: a trade nobody
-- had reported turns up late. Morpholog does not rewrite the price or
-- the statement that used it. It shows which prices no longer match the
-- evidence, which statement needs review, and admits a correction beside
-- the original, which still names what it used and stays lawful on the
-- evidence of its time.

program gas_day_cash_out

-- Who may do what. One operator appoints the others, so every authority
-- below is itself a claim on the record, with a name and a history.
predicate Operator(operator: Subject)
predicate CalendarPublisher(calendar: Subject, publisher: Subject)
predicate ReceiptAuthority(boundary: Subject, receiver: Subject)
predicate MayNominate(nominator: Subject, shipper: Subject)
predicate MarketDataSource(source: Subject)
predicate SettlementAgent(agent: Subject)

-- Not every party is a shipper. A Trader User may trade gas but
-- nominates no flow: the code's nomination rules leave Trader Users out,
-- except for trade nominations (TPD C1.12). The On-the-day Commodity
-- Market (OCM) has an operator, which reports each trade made on it,
-- and a clearer, which stands between the two parties of every one.
predicate TraderUser(party: Subject)
predicate OcmOperator(operator: Subject)
predicate TradingSystemClearer(clearer: Subject)

-- Local days, as `morpholog generate local-days` writes them. Gas days
-- are one calendar (`--at 05:00`); the 13:00 nomination deadlines are
-- another (`--at 13:00`), one row per date. Each row names the zone
-- data it was computed from. Morpholog does not compute a calendar: it
-- keeps the publisher's rows and who vouched for them.
predicate LocalDay(calendar: Subject, local_date: Date, starts_at: Timestamp, ends_at: Timestamp, tzdb_version: Subject, tzdb_digest: Subject)
    unique by (calendar, local_date)
    append only

-- A message the network's system acknowledged, with the instant it did.
-- Only that system's own receiver may admit one, so a late nomination
-- cannot be made to look timely.
predicate Received(message: Subject, boundary: Subject, received_at: Timestamp)
    unique by (message)
    append only
predicate OutputNomination(message: Subject, shipper: Subject, gas_day: Date, qty_kwh: Decimal)
    unique by (message)
    append only

-- A shipper can change its mind during the day: a "renomination" is a
-- new nomination for the same day that takes over from the one in
-- force, from an hour the shipper names. Each one is a nomination of
-- its own, kept forever; the record says which is current and which
-- one each replaced.
predicate Renomination(message: Subject, effective_at: Timestamp)
    unique by (message)
    append only
predicate CurrentNomination(shipper: Subject, gas_day: Date, message: Subject)
    current pointer by (shipper, gas_day)
    superseded via Renominates
predicate Renominates(new_message: Subject, prior_message: Subject)

-- The rate gas is expected to flow at, in kWh an hour (TPD C1.8). It is
-- computed from the nominations, never stored: see `implied_flow_rate`.
predicate ImpliedFlowRate(shipper: Subject, gas_day: Date, kwh_per_hour: Decimal)

-- Two shippers can also trade gas without moving any: one "disposes" of
-- a quantity on a day, the other "acquires" it, and each tells the
-- network in a "trade nomination" naming the other (TPD C5). The two
-- must correspond and arrive close enough together; then the pair is
-- effective, and the quantity moves from one shipper's imbalance to the
-- other's. A side with no counterpart in time is rejected; a pair whose
-- quantities differ is rejected, both sides.
predicate TradeNomination(message: Subject, shipper: Subject, gas_day: Date, side: Subject, counterparty: Subject, qty_kwh: Decimal)
    unique by (message)
    append only
predicate TradeEffective(first: Subject, second: Subject)
    unique by (first)
    unique by (second)
    append only
predicate TradeRejected(message: Subject, reason: Subject)
    unique by (message)
    append only
predicate TradeWithdrawn(message: Subject, withdrawal: Subject)
    unique by (message)
    append only

-- A trade made on the OCM, as its operator's notice reports it: who
-- posted the offer (`originating`), who accepted it, which way the gas
-- goes, how much, and whether it is physical, a trade that also changes
-- the gas a party puts in or takes out. Each of the trade nominations
-- it gives names the notice it came from.
predicate MarketTransaction(notice: Subject, gas_day: Date, originating: Subject, accepting: Subject, originator_sells: Bool, qty_kwh: Decimal, physical: Bool)
    unique by (notice)
    append only
predicate NominatedUnder(nomination: Subject, notice: Subject)
    unique by (nomination)
    append only

-- The network's system answers every message it receives: it takes it
-- as a nomination of some kind, or sets it aside, as malformed, of a
-- kind this example does not model, or refused.
predicate SetAside(message: Subject, reason: Subject)
    unique by (message)
    append only

-- How far the network's system says its record of receipts is complete:
-- every message it received before `until` is on the record. It speaks
-- for one system, never for the world, and only moves forward. It is
-- what lets a trade nomination be rejected for want of a counterpart
-- without a timely counterpart turning up afterwards.
predicate CompleteThrough(boundary: Subject, until: Timestamp)
    append only

-- The calendar can allow what the network's system cannot do. On the
-- night the clocks go back, 25 October 2026, the hour from 01:00 to 02:00
-- happens twice, and National Gas's notice ("Gemini October 2026 Clock
-- Change Supporting Information", slides 4 and 7) says its system takes
-- no renomination or trade nomination in the second one, 01:00 to
-- 01:59:59 GMT, and processes renominations received from 01:55 BST to
-- 02:25 GMT only at 02:25 GMT. The code says none of this, so the
-- notice enters as claims of its own, each naming its source: a stretch
-- the system was unavailable, from `starts` up to `ends`, and one in
-- which it held renominations until `processed_at`.
predicate Unavailable(system: Subject, starts: Timestamp, ends: Timestamp, source: Subject)
    append only
predicate RenominationDeferral(system: Subject, starts: Timestamp, ends: Timestamp, processed_at: Timestamp, source: Subject)
    append only

-- The market's evidence: the day's balancing trades, National Gas's own
-- buy and sell actions, and a statement that the day's actions are all
-- on the record. "No action that day" is only known once that statement
-- exists; before it, the action may simply not have arrived yet.
predicate OcmTrade(trade: Subject, gas_day: Date, qty_kwh: Decimal, price_p_per_kwh: Decimal)
    unique by (trade)
predicate NgBuyAction(action: Subject, gas_day: Date, price_p_per_kwh: Decimal)
    unique by (action)
predicate NgSellAction(action: Subject, gas_day: Date, price_p_per_kwh: Decimal)
    unique by (action)
predicate ActionsComplete(gas_day: Date)
-- The default margin, published once a year for the gas year ahead. A
-- day before any published margin gets no statement: the gaps are
-- intended, so the declaration says `partial`.
predicate DefaultSmp(market: Subject, effective_from: Date, p_per_kwh: Decimal)
    effective by (market) on (effective_from) partial

-- Each SAP is a determination with an identity, and it names exactly
-- the evidence it was computed from: the trades it used, or the seven
-- earlier determinations a quiet day leaned on. The current one for a
-- day can be replaced, but only by a new determination that names the
-- one it replaced; nothing is edited in place.
--
-- It also names the precision it was kept to. The code states none, and
-- a quiet day's mean of seven figures, each carried to every digit a
-- decimal holds, adds up to more digits than one can hold. So each
-- determination is rounded to a stated quantum, halves away from zero,
-- under a named policy: the example's choice, not the code's.
predicate SapPrecision(policy: Subject, quantum_p_per_kwh: Decimal)
    unique by (policy)
    append only
predicate SapDetermination(version: Subject, gas_day: Date, sap_p_per_kwh: Decimal, precision: Subject)
    unique by (version)
    append only
predicate SapUsedTrade(version: Subject, trade: Subject)
    append only
predicate SapUsedPrior(version: Subject, prior: Subject)
    append only
predicate CurrentSap(gas_day: Date, version: Subject)
    current pointer by (gas_day)
    superseded via SapSupersedes
predicate SapSupersedes(new_version: Subject, prior_version: Subject)

-- A shipper's imbalance for a day, in kWh: positive when long, negative
-- when short. It has an identity, so a statement can name the one it used.
predicate Imbalance(imbalance: Subject, shipper: Subject, gas_day: Date, imbalance_kwh: Decimal)
    unique by (imbalance)
    unique by (shipper, gas_day)

-- How a price becomes a charge. The code gives the price formula but no
-- precision to charge at, and a price with many decimals times a large
-- volume is more digits than an exact decimal holds. So the charge uses
-- the price rounded to a stated quantum, halves away from zero. This is
-- the example's own policy, named and versioned, not the code's.
predicate ChargingPolicy(policy: Subject, quantum_p_per_kwh: Decimal)
    unique by (policy)
    append only

-- A cash-out statement names the SAP determination and the imbalance it
-- used, the prices it computed, and the policy and price it charged at.
-- The money is one signed figure: positive when National Gas pays the
-- shipper, negative when the shipper pays. A price can be negative, so
-- the sign cannot be read off the imbalance alone. It is in pence and not
-- rounded to whole pence: no monetary rounding is stated either, so it is
-- a calculated cash flow, not an amount payable. A correction is a new
-- statement that names the one it corrects; the original stays.
predicate Statement(statement: Subject, shipper: Subject, gas_day: Date, sap_version: Subject, imbalance: Subject, smp_buy: Decimal, smp_sell: Decimal, policy: Subject, charging_price: Decimal, cash_flow_to_shipper_pence: Decimal)
    unique by (statement)
    append only
predicate CurrentStatement(shipper: Subject, gas_day: Date, statement: Subject)
    current pointer by (shipper, gas_day)
    superseded via StatementCorrects
predicate StatementCorrects(new_statement: Subject, prior_statement: Subject)
predicate NeedsReview(statement: Subject)


-- A determination is stale when the evidence now on the record differs
-- from what it names: a trade for its day it did not use (a late trade),
-- a trade it used that is no longer held (withdrawn or corrected), or an
-- earlier determination it leaned on that is no longer that day's
-- current one.
define directly_stale(v):
    (SapDetermination(v, day, _, _) and OcmTrade(t, day, _, _) and not SapUsedTrade(v, t))
    or (SapUsedTrade(v, t) and not OcmTrade(t, _, _, _))
    or (SapUsedPrior(v, p) and SapDetermination(p, d, _, _) and not CurrentSap(d, p))

-- What a quiet day's SAP leaned on, however far back: its seven, their
-- seven, and so on. The links form a graph, and this follows every
-- branch of it.
define depends_on(v, prior):
    SapUsedPrior(v, prior)
    or (SapUsedPrior(v, between) and depends_on(between, prior))

define stale(v):
    directly_stale(v) or (depends_on(v, w) and directly_stale(w))

-- A trade nomination still waiting: not part of an effective pair, not
-- rejected, not withdrawn.
define trade_pending(m):
    TradeNomination(message: m, ..) and not TradeEffective(m, _) and not TradeEffective(_, m) and not TradeRejected(m, _) and not TradeWithdrawn(m, _)

-- Whether the network has read a message: taken it as a nomination, a
-- trade nomination or a withdrawal, or set it aside. Each act that reads
-- a message refuses one already read (`unread`), so a message is read
-- once and one set aside never becomes a nomination afterwards.
define message_read(m):
    OutputNomination(message: m, ..) or TradeNomination(message: m, ..) or TradeWithdrawn(_, m) or SetAside(m, _) or MarketTransaction(notice: m, ..)

-- Whether the system was unavailable at an instant, and whether it has
-- already read a renomination, or a trade nomination, received in a
-- stretch.
define unavailable(system, at):
    Unavailable(system, starts, ends, _) and starts at_or_before at and at strictly_before ends

define renomination_read_in(system, starts, ends):
    Received(m, system, at) and starts at_or_before at and at strictly_before ends and Renomination(m, _)

define trade_nomination_read_in(system, starts, ends):
    Received(m, system, at) and starts at_or_before at and at strictly_before ends and (TradeNomination(message: m, ..) or MarketTransaction(notice: m, ..))

-- When the counterpart to a trade nomination received at `at` is due
-- (TPD C5.2.3(b)): by 06:00 on the gas day if the first side came before
-- the day started, which is 06:00 on the day's own date, an hour into it;
-- otherwise within the hour after it.
define counterpart_due(at, day_starts, six_am) = (if(at strictly_before day_starts, six_am, at + duration(PT60M)))

-- Two trade nominations that correspond (C5.1.1, C5.2.1): the same day,
-- opposite sides, each naming the other's shipper.
define trades_correspond(a, b):
    TradeNomination(a, shipper, gas_day, side, counterparty, _) and TradeNomination(b, counterparty, gas_day, other_side, shipper, _) and not (other_side = side)

-- The later of two trade nominations arrived in time for the earlier
-- (C5.2.3(b)). "Within 60 minutes before or after" is the same either
-- way round, so the earlier one sets the deadline.
define received_in_time(a, b):
    TradeNomination(message: a, gas_day: gas_day, ..) and Received(a, #gemini, a_at) and Received(b, #gemini, b_at) and LocalDay(#gas_day, gas_day, day_starts, _, _, _) and LocalDay(#trade_counterpart_deadline, gas_day, six_am, _, _, _) and ((a_at at_or_before b_at and b_at at_or_before counterpart_due(a_at, day_starts, six_am)) or (b_at strictly_before a_at and a_at at_or_before counterpart_due(b_at, day_starts, six_am)))

-- The implied flow rate of a shipper's nomination for a day (TPD C1.8).
-- A first nomination flows evenly over 24 hours, even on a 23- or
-- 25-hour day: the code divides by 24 whatever the day's length. Each
-- renomination then adds its change in quantity, spread over the hours
-- left from its effective hour to the end of the day. Renominations
-- form one chain per shipper and day, each replacing the one current
-- when it came, so the rate is the first quantity's share plus every
-- change since, summed. The code states no precision for the rate, and
-- a decimal cannot hold a long quotient added to a rate, so the example
-- states one, `example_flow_rate_precision_v1`: each term to six decimal
-- places of kWh an hour, halves away from zero, before they are added.
define implied_flow_rate(shipper, gas_day) = (sum(round(q / 24, 0.000001) | OutputNomination(m, shipper, gas_day, q) and not Renomination(m, _)) + sum(round((q - prior_q) / ((day_ends - e) / duration(PT1H)), 0.000001) | Renomination(m, e) and OutputNomination(m, shipper, gas_day, q) and Renominates(m, prior) and OutputNomination(prior, _, _, prior_q) and LocalDay(#gas_day, gas_day, _, day_ends, _, _)))


-- Set-up. The first operator appoints itself once; the operator then
-- names who publishes each calendar, which system's receiver vouches for
-- receipts, who may nominate for whom, where market data comes from,
-- and who determines prices and issues statements.
transformation appoint_operator(operator):
    require no_operator_yet: not Operator(_)
    admit Operator(operator)

transformation appoint_calendar_publisher(calendar, publisher):
    require operator: Operator(actor)
    admit CalendarPublisher(calendar, publisher)

transformation appoint_receipt_authority(boundary, receiver):
    require operator: Operator(actor)
    admit ReceiptAuthority(boundary, receiver)

transformation grant_nomination(nominator, shipper):
    require operator: Operator(actor)
    admit MayNominate(nominator, shipper)

transformation appoint_market_data_source(source):
    require operator: Operator(actor)
    admit MarketDataSource(source)

transformation appoint_settlement_agent(agent):
    require operator: Operator(actor)
    admit SettlementAgent(agent)

transformation register_trader_user(party):
    require operator: Operator(actor)
    admit TraderUser(party)

transformation appoint_ocm_operator(operator):
    require operator: Operator(actor)
    admit OcmOperator(operator)

transformation appoint_clearer(clearer):
    require operator: Operator(actor)
    require one_clearer: not TradingSystemClearer(_)
    admit TradingSystemClearer(clearer)

transformation adopt_sap_precision(policy, quantum_p_per_kwh: Decimal):
    require operator: Operator(actor)
    require positive_quantum: 0 < quantum_p_per_kwh
    admit SapPrecision(policy, quantum_p_per_kwh)

transformation adopt_charging_policy(policy, quantum_p_per_kwh: Decimal):
    require operator: Operator(actor)
    require positive_quantum: 0 < quantum_p_per_kwh
    admit ChargingPolicy(policy, quantum_p_per_kwh)

-- One row of a calendar, exactly as the generator wrote it.
transformation publish_local_day(calendar, local_date: Date, starts_at: Timestamp, ends_at: Timestamp, tzdb_version, tzdb_digest):
    require publisher: CalendarPublisher(calendar, actor)
    admit LocalDay(calendar, local_date, starts_at, ends_at, tzdb_version, tzdb_digest)


-- The network's system acknowledges a message. Only its own receiver
-- may say so: a shipper cannot admit a receipt for itself. Nor may the
-- receiver record a receipt before an instant it has already said its
-- record is complete through: a decision taken on that word would no
-- longer rest on what it said.
transformation receive(message, boundary, received_at: Timestamp):
    require receiver: ReceiptAuthority(boundary, actor)
    require record_open: not (CompleteThrough(boundary, until) and received_at strictly_before until)
    admit Received(message, boundary, received_at)

-- A daily-metered output nomination (TPD C1.2, C2.3.1): due by 13:00 on
-- the day before the gas day. The deadline is read from the published
-- 13:00 row for that date, never typed in by whoever nominates, and
-- lateness is judged on the receipt from the network's own system,
-- Gemini, not on when this act runs or on a receipt from anywhere else.
transformation nominate_output(message, shipper, gas_day: Date, qty_kwh: Decimal):
    require may_nominate: MayNominate(actor, shipper)
    require not_a_trader_user: not TraderUser(shipper)
    require gas_day_published: LocalDay(#gas_day, gas_day, _, _, _, _)
    require received_by_the_network: Received(message, #gemini, _)
    bind Received(message, #gemini, received_at)
    require unread: not message_read(message)
    let preceding_day = gas_day - span(P1D)
    bind LocalDay(#dm_output_deadline, preceding_day, due, _, _, _)
    require in_time: received_at strictly_before due
    require first_for_the_day: not CurrentNomination(shipper, gas_day, _)
    admit OutputNomination(message, shipper, gas_day, qty_kwh)
    admit CurrentNomination(shipper, gas_day, message)

-- A renomination (TPD C4.1). It names the nomination it revises (C4.1.5(a)),
-- and that must be the one in force: a message judged after a later one
-- has already replaced its nomination is refused, whatever order the
-- network received them in, and so is one naming a nomination received
-- after it. Once admitted it replaces that nomination at once, though
-- the one it replaced still governs the flow until the new one takes
-- effect (C4.1.8). Its receipt falls between 15:00 London the day before
-- and 03:00 on the gas day itself, which falls on the next calendar
-- date, since the day runs to 05:00 (C1.2, C4.1.3(b)). Both instants are
-- read from published rows. The shipper names the hour it takes effect:
-- inside the day, on the hour (C1.9), and at least an hour after the
-- network received it (C4.1.10(a)). One received while the system was
-- unavailable is refused as unavailable, not as late. One the system
-- held back counts its hour from when it was processed, since the
-- network had no notice of it before (`example_deferred_lead_time_v1`).
-- The code does not say what a
-- renomination taking effect before the one it replaces does to that
-- one; this example refuses it (`example_renomination_order_v1`), so the
-- nominations in force take effect in the order they replace each other.
-- And it may not imply gas flowing backwards: a cut so deep, so late,
-- that the hours left would need a negative rate is refused (C4.1.3(d)).
transformation renominate(message, shipper, gas_day: Date, previous, effective_at: Timestamp, qty_kwh: Decimal):
    require may_nominate: MayNominate(actor, shipper)
    require not_a_trader_user: not TraderUser(shipper)
    require received_by_the_network: Received(message, #gemini, _)
    bind Received(message, #gemini, received_at)
    require unread: not message_read(message)
    require earlier_nomination: CurrentNomination(shipper, gas_day, _)
    require previous_in_force: CurrentNomination(shipper, gas_day, previous)
    bind OutputNomination(previous, shipper, gas_day, prior_qty)
    bind Received(previous, #gemini, previous_received)
    require after_the_previous: previous_received strictly_before received_at
    let preceding_day = gas_day - span(P1D)
    let next_date = gas_day + span(P1D)
    bind LocalDay(#renomination_start, preceding_day, opens, _, _, _)
    bind LocalDay(#renomination_end, next_date, closes, _, _, _)
    require in_window: opens at_or_before received_at and received_at at_or_before closes
    require available: not unavailable(#gemini, received_at)
    bind LocalDay(#gas_day, gas_day, day_starts, day_ends, _, _)
    require within_the_day: day_starts at_or_before effective_at and effective_at strictly_before day_ends
    require on_the_hour: ((effective_at - day_starts) / duration(PT1H)) % 1 = 0
    require lead_time: received_at + duration(PT60M) at_or_before effective_at
    require lead_after_processing: not (RenominationDeferral(#gemini, starts, ends, processed, _) and starts at_or_before received_at and received_at strictly_before ends and effective_at strictly_before processed + duration(PT60M))
    require in_order: not Renomination(previous, _) or (Renomination(previous, previous_effective) and previous_effective at_or_before effective_at)
    require rate_not_negative: implied_flow_rate(shipper, gas_day) + round((qty_kwh - prior_qty) / ((day_ends - effective_at) / duration(PT1H)), 0.000001) >= 0
    admit OutputNomination(message, shipper, gas_day, qty_kwh)
    admit Renomination(message, effective_at)
    retract CurrentNomination(shipper, gas_day, previous)
    admit Renominates(message, previous)
    admit CurrentNomination(shipper, gas_day, message)


-- A trade nomination (TPD C5.2). It names the day, its side, the
-- shipper on the other side and the quantity. It may be made from the
-- start of the gas day thirty days before to 03:00 on the gas day,
-- judged on its receipt, between two different shippers, and not while
-- the system was unavailable.
transformation trade_nominate(message, shipper, gas_day: Date, side, counterparty, qty_kwh: Decimal):
    require may_nominate: MayNominate(actor, shipper)
    require received_by_the_network: Received(message, #gemini, _)
    bind Received(message, #gemini, received_at)
    require unread: not message_read(message)
    require known_side: side = #disposing or side = #acquiring
    require two_shippers: not (counterparty = shipper)
    let opening_day = gas_day - span(P30D)
    let next_date = gas_day + span(P1D)
    bind LocalDay(#gas_day, opening_day, opens, _, _, _)
    bind LocalDay(#renomination_end, next_date, closes, _, _, _)
    require in_window: opens at_or_before received_at and received_at at_or_before closes
    require available: not unavailable(#gemini, received_at)
    admit TradeNomination(message, shipper, gas_day, side, counterparty, qty_kwh)

-- The network's system pairs two waiting trade nominations. They must
-- correspond, and the later must arrive in time for the earlier. Then
-- the pair is effective, or, if the quantities differ, both sides are
-- rejected (C5.2.3(c)). A trade nomination names no trade, so with
-- several sides waiting the network chooses the pair; but it may not
-- reject a pair for unequal quantities while either side has an equal
-- partner waiting in time, which would discard a pair the code makes
-- effective. That limit is this example's (`example_trade_pairing_v1`).
transformation match_trades(first, second):
    require network: ReceiptAuthority(#gemini, actor)
    require both_waiting: trade_pending(first) and trade_pending(second)
    require corresponding: trades_correspond(first, second)
    require in_time: received_in_time(first, second)
    bind TradeNomination(message: first, qty_kwh: first_qty, ..)
    bind TradeNomination(message: second, qty_kwh: second_qty, ..)
    require no_equal_pair: first_qty = second_qty or not ((trades_correspond(first, o) and not (o = second) and trade_pending(o) and received_in_time(first, o) and TradeNomination(message: o, qty_kwh: first_qty, ..)) or (trades_correspond(second, o) and not (o = first) and trade_pending(o) and received_in_time(second, o) and TradeNomination(message: o, qty_kwh: second_qty, ..)))
    if first_qty = second_qty:
        admit TradeEffective(first, second)
    else:
        admit TradeRejected(first, #quantity_mismatch)
        admit TradeRejected(second, #quantity_mismatch)

-- A waiting trade nomination with no counterpart in time is rejected
-- (C5.2.3(b)). But only once the network's system reports its record
-- complete past the counterpart's deadline, so no timely counterpart can
-- still arrive, and only if no waiting counterpart came in time. That
-- evidence rule is this example's, not a clause. Complete through an
-- instant covers what came before it, and a counterpart at the deadline
-- exactly is in time, so the record must be complete past the deadline.
-- A complete record of receipts is not yet a complete record of trade
-- nominations: a message received in time may not have been read. So
-- every message received by the deadline must have been read
-- (`example_all_receipts_read_v1`), and one read is never read again.
-- A trade on the OCM. The market's operator reports it in one notice
-- and, by its role in the market rather than a mandate from either
-- party, makes the trade nominations for both sides: on the clearer's
-- account, and as each party's agent (TPD D2.2.5, D2.2.6). The clearer
-- is the counterparty to each party separately (D2.2.1(a)), so one trade
-- is two pairs: the seller disposing to the clearer, and the clearer
-- disposing to the buyer. The operator vouches for what the notice says;
-- the network's receiver, only for when it arrived.
--
-- Submitted is not yet effective: the code makes a pair effective when
-- its two sides correspond and arrive in time. Here the four are built
-- together from one notice, with one quantity and one receipt, so each
-- pair corresponds and is in time by construction, and both pairs are
-- effective in this act. There is nothing left for the network to match.
--
-- A Trader User may accept a physical offer but not post one (Annex D-1
-- 4.9(b)). The market's other conditions on a physical trade, such as
-- the hour's lead time for posting it (D2.2.7), are the market's to
-- check before it reports the trade: this records a trade already made.
-- A positive quantity is this example's reading; the code does not say.
transformation report_market_transaction(notice, gas_day: Date, originating, accepting, originator_sells: Bool, qty_kwh: Decimal, physical: Bool):
    require operator: OcmOperator(actor)
    require received_by_the_network: Received(notice, #gemini, _)
    bind Received(notice, #gemini, received_at)
    require unread: not message_read(notice)
    bind clearer_appointed: TradingSystemClearer(clearer)
    require two_participants: not (originating = accepting) and not (originating = clearer) and not (accepting = clearer)
    require positive_quantity: 0 < qty_kwh
    let opening_day = gas_day - span(P30D)
    let next_date = gas_day + span(P1D)
    bind LocalDay(#gas_day, opening_day, opens, _, _, _)
    bind LocalDay(#renomination_end, next_date, closes, _, _, _)
    require in_window: opens at_or_before received_at and received_at at_or_before closes
    require available: not unavailable(#gemini, received_at)
    require trader_user_does_not_post_physical: not (physical = true and TraderUser(originating))
    let seller = if(originator_sells = true, originating, accepting)
    let buyer = if(originator_sells = true, accepting, originating)
    let seller_disposes = new Subject()
    let clearer_acquires = new Subject()
    let clearer_disposes = new Subject()
    let buyer_acquires = new Subject()
    admit MarketTransaction(notice, gas_day, originating, accepting, originator_sells, qty_kwh, physical)
    admit TradeNomination(seller_disposes, seller, gas_day, #disposing, clearer, qty_kwh)
    admit TradeNomination(clearer_acquires, clearer, gas_day, #acquiring, seller, qty_kwh)
    admit TradeNomination(clearer_disposes, clearer, gas_day, #disposing, buyer, qty_kwh)
    admit TradeNomination(buyer_acquires, buyer, gas_day, #acquiring, clearer, qty_kwh)
    admit NominatedUnder(seller_disposes, notice)
    admit NominatedUnder(clearer_acquires, notice)
    admit NominatedUnder(clearer_disposes, notice)
    admit NominatedUnder(buyer_acquires, notice)
    admit TradeEffective(seller_disposes, clearer_acquires)
    admit TradeEffective(clearer_disposes, buyer_acquires)

transformation expire_trade(message):
    require network: ReceiptAuthority(#gemini, actor)
    require waiting: trade_pending(message)
    bind TradeNomination(message: message, gas_day: gas_day, ..)
    bind Received(message, #gemini, received_at)
    bind LocalDay(#gas_day, gas_day, day_starts, _, _, _)
    bind LocalDay(#trade_counterpart_deadline, gas_day, six_am, _, _, _)
    let due = counterpart_due(received_at, day_starts, six_am)
    require record_complete: CompleteThrough(#gemini, until) and due strictly_before until
    require every_receipt_read: not (Received(m, #gemini, at) and at at_or_before due and not message_read(m))
    require no_counterpart_in_time: not (trades_correspond(message, o) and trade_pending(o) and received_in_time(message, o))
    admit TradeRejected(message, #no_counterpart)

-- A trade nomination withdrawn by its shipper's nominator, any time
-- before the gas day and never within it (C5.2.4). The withdrawal is a
-- message of its own, judged on its receipt. The code also allows
-- withdrawing one side of a pair already effective, before the day, but
-- does not say what becomes of the other side; this example does not
-- model it and refuses. Nothing amends a trade nomination here: the code
-- allows no amendment within the day and says nothing of one before it.
transformation withdraw_trade(message, withdrawal):
    bind TradeNomination(message, shipper, gas_day, _, _, _)
    require may_nominate: MayNominate(actor, shipper)
    require received_by_the_network: Received(withdrawal, #gemini, _)
    bind Received(withdrawal, #gemini, withdrawn_at)
    require unread: not message_read(withdrawal)
    require standing: not TradeRejected(message, _) and not TradeWithdrawn(message, _)
    bind LocalDay(#gas_day, gas_day, day_starts, _, _, _)
    require before_the_day: withdrawn_at strictly_before day_starts
    require unpaired: not TradeEffective(message, _) and not TradeEffective(_, message)
    admit TradeWithdrawn(message, withdrawal)

-- The network's system sets a message aside, once, and only one it has
-- not read already.
transformation set_aside(message, reason):
    bind Received(message, boundary, _)
    require receiver: ReceiptAuthority(boundary, actor)
    require unread: not message_read(message)
    admit SetAside(message, reason)

-- The network's system reports how far its record is complete. It only
-- moves forward.
transformation report_complete_through(boundary, until: Timestamp):
    require receiver: ReceiptAuthority(boundary, actor)
    require forward: not (CompleteThrough(boundary, u) and until at_or_before u)
    admit CompleteThrough(boundary, until)

-- The network's system announces an outage, or a stretch in which it
-- holds renominations back, before it has read any message it governs
-- received in it: one already taken in that stretch would contradict
-- the announcement. An outage governs renominations and trade
-- nominations; a deferral only changes when renominations are
-- processed, so a trade nomination read in its stretch is no obstacle.
-- National Gas published its notice three weeks ahead.
transformation announce_outage(system, starts: Timestamp, ends: Timestamp, source):
    require receiver: ReceiptAuthority(system, actor)
    require nothing_read_in_it: not renomination_read_in(system, starts, ends) and not trade_nomination_read_in(system, starts, ends)
    admit Unavailable(system, starts, ends, source)

transformation announce_deferral(system, starts: Timestamp, ends: Timestamp, processed_at: Timestamp, source):
    require receiver: ReceiptAuthority(system, actor)
    require nothing_read_in_it: not renomination_read_in(system, starts, ends)
    admit RenominationDeferral(system, starts, ends, processed_at, source)


-- Market data, from its appointed source only.
transformation report_trade(trade, gas_day: Date, qty_kwh: Decimal, price_p_per_kwh: Decimal):
    require source: MarketDataSource(actor)
    require positive_quantity: 0 < qty_kwh
    admit OcmTrade(trade, gas_day, qty_kwh, price_p_per_kwh)

transformation report_buy_action(action, gas_day: Date, price_p_per_kwh: Decimal):
    require source: MarketDataSource(actor)
    admit NgBuyAction(action, gas_day, price_p_per_kwh)

transformation report_sell_action(action, gas_day: Date, price_p_per_kwh: Decimal):
    require source: MarketDataSource(actor)
    admit NgSellAction(action, gas_day, price_p_per_kwh)

transformation report_actions_complete(gas_day: Date):
    require source: MarketDataSource(actor)
    admit ActionsComplete(gas_day)

transformation publish_default_smp(market, effective_from: Date, p_per_kwh: Decimal):
    require source: MarketDataSource(actor)
    admit DefaultSmp(market, effective_from, p_per_kwh)

transformation record_imbalance(imbalance, shipper, gas_day: Date, imbalance_kwh: Decimal):
    require agent: SettlementAgent(actor)
    admit Imbalance(imbalance, shipper, gas_day, imbalance_kwh)


-- SAP for a day with trades (F1.2.1(c)): the sum of each trade's
-- quantity times its price, over the sum of the quantities. The act takes
-- no price: it computes the figure from the trades on the record and
-- names every one it used, kept to the precision policy it is given. A
-- day's first determination needs no predecessor; a new one may replace
-- the current one only when that one has gone stale.
transformation determine_sap(gas_day: Date, precision):
    require agent: SettlementAgent(actor)
    require traded: OcmTrade(gas_day: gas_day, ..)
    require first_or_stale: not CurrentSap(gas_day, _) or (CurrentSap(gas_day, current) and stale(current))
    bind SapPrecision(precision, quantum)
    let sap = round(sum(q * p | OcmTrade(_, gas_day, q, p)) / sum(q | OcmTrade(_, gas_day, q, _)), quantum)
    let version = new Subject()
    admit SapDetermination(version, gas_day, sap, precision)
    for OcmTrade(trade, gas_day, _, _):
        admit SapUsedTrade(version, trade)
    for CurrentSap(gas_day, prior):
        retract CurrentSap(gas_day, prior)
        admit SapSupersedes(version, prior)
    admit CurrentSap(gas_day, version)

-- SAP for a day with no trades (F1.2.2): the mean of the current SAP of
-- each of the seven days before it, whichever rule determined them. It
-- names all seven, and is kept to the precision policy it is given. It
-- may not lean on a day whose own figure is already stale: that day is
-- re-determined first, or the new figure would be stale on arrival.
transformation determine_fallback_sap(gas_day: Date, precision):
    require agent: SettlementAgent(actor)
    require no_trades: not OcmTrade(gas_day: gas_day, ..)
    require first_or_stale: not CurrentSap(gas_day, _) or (CurrentSap(gas_day, current) and stale(current))
    let start = gas_day - span(P7D)
    require seven_preceding_days: sum(1 | CurrentSap(d, _) and start on_or_before d and d before gas_day) = 7
    require inputs_current: not (CurrentSap(d, v) and start on_or_before d and d before gas_day and stale(v))
    bind SapPrecision(precision, quantum)
    let sap = round(sum(p | CurrentSap(d, v) and SapDetermination(v, d, p, _) and start on_or_before d and d before gas_day) / 7, quantum)
    let version = new Subject()
    admit SapDetermination(version, gas_day, sap, precision)
    for CurrentSap(d, v):
        if start on_or_before d and d before gas_day:
            admit SapUsedPrior(version, v)
    for CurrentSap(gas_day, prior):
        retract CurrentSap(gas_day, prior)
        admit SapSupersedes(version, prior)
    admit CurrentSap(gas_day, version)


-- A cash-out statement (F1.2.1(a), (b); F2.3.1, F2.3.3). SMP Buy is the
-- greater of SAP plus the default margin and National Gas's highest buy
-- price that day; SMP Sell the lesser of SAP minus the margin and its
-- lowest sell price. The code does not say what the second price is on a
-- day National Gas took no action. This example reads it as absent, and
-- only once the day's actions are known to be complete. A long shipper
-- is paid its surplus at the Sell price, a short one pays its shortfall
-- at the Buy price, each at the charging policy's price. A statement may
-- not use a SAP the record already knows is stale.
transformation issue_statement(shipper, gas_day: Date, policy):
    require agent: SettlementAgent(actor)
    require actions_complete: ActionsComplete(gas_day)
    require first_or_stale: not CurrentStatement(shipper, gas_day, _) or (CurrentStatement(shipper, gas_day, current) and Statement(statement: current, sap_version: used, ..) and stale(used))
    bind CurrentSap(gas_day, version)
    require sap_not_stale: not stale(version)
    bind SapDetermination(version, gas_day, sap, _)
    bind default_smp_in_force_on(#nbp, gas_day, margin)
    bind Imbalance(imbalance_id, shipper, gas_day, imbalance)
    bind ChargingPolicy(policy, quantum)
    let smp_buy = if(NgBuyAction(_, gas_day, _), max(sap + margin, max(b | NgBuyAction(_, gas_day, b))), sap + margin)
    let smp_sell = if(NgSellAction(_, gas_day, _), min(sap - margin, min(s | NgSellAction(_, gas_day, s))), sap - margin)
    let charging_price = if(0 < imbalance, round(smp_sell, quantum), round(smp_buy, quantum))
    let cash_flow = imbalance * charging_price
    let statement = new Subject()
    admit Statement(statement, shipper, gas_day, version, imbalance_id, smp_buy, smp_sell, policy, charging_price, cash_flow)
    for CurrentStatement(shipper, gas_day, prior):
        retract CurrentStatement(shipper, gas_day, prior)
        admit StatementCorrects(statement, prior)
    admit CurrentStatement(shipper, gas_day, statement)


-- Which current statements need review: those whose SAP no longer
-- matches the evidence, directly or through the days it leaned on. It is
-- read, never stored, so it can never fall out of step with the record.
derived NeedsReview(statement):
    over CurrentStatement(_, _, statement) and Statement(statement: statement, sap_version: used, ..) and stale(used)

-- The rate the nomination in force implies, read from the chain that led
-- to it: the rate from its effective hour to the end of the day. Before
-- that hour the nomination it replaced still applies, at its own rate.
derived ImpliedFlowRate(shipper, gas_day):
    over CurrentNomination(shipper, gas_day, _)
    value kwh_per_hour = implied_flow_rate(shipper, gas_day)
